<article class="essay">
  <header class="essay-intro">
    <p class="essay-kicker">history of English · September 2026</p>
    <h1>How Final <em>e</em> Made Different Histories Look Alike</h1>
    <p class="essay-deck">A nine-lexel PLAEME study of a spelling pattern that regularised more than one vowel history</p>
    <p class="essay-byline">Hanpu Li</p>
    <p class="essay-note essay-provenance" role="note">Developed from a 2021 squib and a 2022 History of English paper; the corpus study and argument are new to this version.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="essay-body">
    <p><em>Name</em> and <em>time</em> now teach the same lesson. In both words, a vowel followed by one consonant and final <em>e</em> receives what schoolbooks call its long value. The lesson works. It also makes the two words look as if they arrived together.</p>

    <p>They did not. Old English <em>nama</em> had a short stressed vowel and a final vowel; <em>tīma</em> already had a long stressed vowel. The first word belongs to a disputed Middle English history of lengthening and final-vowel loss. The second did not need that event to acquire length. Their present agreement is not a remnant of one shared origin. It is the achievement of a later spelling system that learned to treat unlike histories as the same reading problem.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-1" href="#final-e-note-1" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 1">1</a></sup></p>

    <p>A two-word contrast can expose the mistake, but not tell us whether it is typical. I therefore tested a small, fixed sample against the Parsed Linguistic Atlas of Early Middle English (PLAEME). The result showed a pattern without making final <em>e</em> a transparent record of vowel history. The letter’s distribution was unstable and already crossed the historical distinction a modern reader is tempted to recover from it.</p>

    <section aria-labelledby="final-e-sample-heading">
      <h2 id="final-e-sample-heading">The sample was fixed before the count</h2>

      <p>PLAEME is built from diplomatic transcriptions in the <em>Linguistic Atlas of Early Middle English</em>. Its working release contains 68 files and 172,624 words dated 1250–1325. The unit needs care: a LAEME file represents a scribal language, not necessarily one literary text, manuscript or independent speaker. PLAEME preserves the manuscript detail of LAEME while adding syntactic annotation and searchable “lexels”, labels that usually work like lemmas but are not identical to them.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-2" href="#final-e-note-2" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 2">2</a></sup></p>

      <p>Before counting, I selected nine modern-English lexels. Four have short-vowel disyllabic sources relevant to accounts of Middle English lengthening: <em>name</em>, <em>nose</em>, <em>hope</em> and <em>tale</em>. Five reach the modern final-<em>e</em> pattern from inherited long vowels or other structures: <em>time</em>, <em>home</em>, <em>stone</em>, <em>life</em> and <em>wife</em>. Bosworth–Toller records the long vowels directly in <em>tīma</em>, <em>hām</em>, <em>stān</em>, <em>līf</em> and <em>wīf</em>. The sample is contrastive, not a random draw from the lexicon.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-3" href="#final-e-note-3" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 3">3</a></sup></p>

      <p>I searched all 68 parsed files for those exact lexels, retaining singular common nouns tagged <code>N</code> or <code>N-RH</code> and excluding explicit genitives and plurals. A token counted as final-<em>e</em> when its diplomatic word form ended in <em>e</em> after removing only corpus control marks for morpheme boundaries, insertions, line breaks and abbreviations. I did not modernise the spelling: <em>tyme</em> counted, <em>tim</em> did not. The method avoids a modern-string search that would merge unrelated forms, but exact lexels can miss tokens indexed under another historical label. The totals describe this query and this release, not population prevalence.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-4" href="#final-e-note-4" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 4">4</a></sup></p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="final-e-result-heading">
      <h2 id="final-e-result-heading">The division appears, then fails to hold</h2>

      <p>The short-vowel group favours final <em>e</em>. The query returned <em>name</em> with final <em>e</em> in 107 of 132 tokens, <em>nose</em> in 3 of 5, <em>hope</em> in 14 of 26 and <em>tale</em> in 36 of 50. Across these selected lexels, that is 160 of 213 tokens. The inherited-long group uses final <em>e</em> less often overall: 260 of 691 tokens.</p>

      <p>It is not a historical label. <em>Time</em>, with its inherited long vowel, takes final <em>e</em> in 130 of 169 tokens, almost the same proportion as <em>name</em>. <em>Home</em> has it in only 5 of 70, and <em>stone</em> in 9 of 60. The monosyllabic <em>life</em> is split 92 to 186; <em>wife</em>, 24 to 90. If the letter uniquely marked a vowel newly lengthened through loss of a following syllable, <em>time</em> should not sit beside <em>name</em>, while <em>life</em> and <em>wife</em> should have little reason to enter the pattern at all.</p>

      <p>The instability is not only between words or regions. Sixteen of the 34 PLAEME files containing the lexel <em>life</em> use both final-<em>e</em> and non-final-<em>e</em> forms. Seven of the 18 files containing <em>wife</em> do the same. Smaller mixed counts occur for <em>name</em>, <em>hope</em>, <em>tale</em>, <em>time</em> and <em>stone</em>. A scribal source can therefore vary within one lexel; aggregating a file into a single regional form would destroy evidence rather than summarise it.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-5" href="#final-e-note-5" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 5">5</a></sup></p>

      <p>LAEME’s own documentation warns that some final <em>e</em>s are not etymologically justified and may be ornamental or “decorative”. That category cannot be mechanically assigned to each token in this sample. It does show why presence alone is weak evidence for a pronounced final vowel. Orthography had resources of its own: copying practice, analogy, abbreviation, metre and scribal preference could preserve or supply a letter after its relation to speech had become uncertain.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-6" href="#final-e-note-6" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 6">6</a></sup></p>

      <p>These counts complicate both simple accounts. The selected short-vowel inputs take final <em>e</em> more often, so its distribution was not random. Yet the letter was not a faithful tag for one phonological history: the categories overlap, and individual scribal files mix forms. The later reading convention regularised a distribution that already contained both historical signal and historical ambiguity.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="final-e-neighbours-heading">
      <h2 id="final-e-neighbours-heading"><em>Name</em> and <em>time</em> become neighbours</h2>

      <p>This changes the familiar description of spelling as an archive. An archive is often imagined as a store in which an old form waits to be decoded. The PLAEME forms show something more active. Written conventions preserve, vary and regroup. By the time modern readers encounter <em>name</em> and <em>time</em>, the orthography no longer sorts words by the origin of vowel length. It sorts them by a present relation between a vowel graph, an intervening consonant and a non-syllabic final letter.</p>

      <p>Psycholinguistic evidence explains why the regrouping is useful. Robert Wiley and colleagues’ English Sublexical Toolkit measures graded spelling–sound consistency across more than 13,000 words. They prefer “final E” to “silent E” because an unpronounced letter can still carry information. Readers learn distributions across families of words; <em>name</em>, <em>same</em> and <em>tame</em> can support a pronunciation for a new string without sharing every stage of etymology.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-7" href="#final-e-note-7" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 7">7</a></sup></p>

      <p>The modern pattern is therefore not a degraded historical record. It is a different organisation of the evidence. Its success depends on suppressing distinctions a historian needs. <em>Name</em> and <em>time</em> become useful neighbours when their separate routes cease to matter for reading. The final letter predicts something precisely because the system has made provenance secondary.</p>

      <p>Metre shows the same divergence at a smaller scale. Harvard’s Chaucer materials discuss the line “hire browe broune, hire eye blake” from <em>Alysoun</em>. Every content string is written with final <em>e</em>, but pronouncing all five as syllables overfills the line. The metre supports syllabic endings in <em>browe</em> and <em>eye</em>, not in <em>hire</em>, <em>broune</em> and <em>blake</em>. Written identity coexists with prosodic difference inside one line. Even there, metre constrains rather than records speech: elision, dialect and editorial mediation remain possible.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-8" href="#final-e-note-8" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 8">8</a></sup></p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="final-e-dispute-heading">
      <h2 id="final-e-dispute-heading">The sound change needs different evidence</h2>

      <p><em>Name</em> contains a live disagreement about why its stressed vowel became long. The traditional account, Middle English Open Syllable Lengthening (MEOSL), treats a short stressed vowel in an open syllable as the relevant environment. Myungsook Kim’s analysis makes the change conditional on weakening of the following vowel and sensitive to dialect and chronology. Donka Minkova and Michael Lefkowitz propose Middle English Compensatory Lengthening (MECL): deletion of final schwa preserves prosodic weight by lengthening the stressed vowel.<sup class="essay-noteref"><a id="final-e-ref-9" href="#final-e-note-9" role="doc-noteref" aria-label="Note 9">9</a></sup></p>

      <p>The disagreement is causal, not terminological. In one account syllable structure conditions lengthening; in the other length compensates for deletion. The written final <em>e</em> cannot select between them, because the same surface letter is compatible with a pronounced ending, an ending in loss, a retained spelling and later analogy. The PLAEME count strengthens that warning. It shows the letter crossing into lexels whose long vowels have another history while remaining variable in the very lexels for which lengthening is at issue.</p>

      <p>Nor can the count itself decide the dispute. These are orthographic tokens from a restricted period, selected through nine lexels with unequal frequencies and uneven regional representation. They do not measure vowel duration. A larger study would stratify dates and localisations, model source-level variation, test alternative lexel assignments and compare rhyme or metre where available. Later corpora would be needed to trace when the heterogeneous forms became the more regular modern convention.</p>

      <p>The school rule survives because English spelling eventually brought several vowel histories under the same reading convention. Final <em>e</em> no longer preserves one ancient event. It joins words that sound history must separate again. That regularity helps a reader, but it weakens the letter as evidence for how any particular word arrived there.</p>
    </section>

